# Aberration — Every system has a pattern.

Anomaly detection for physical and digital systems.

Detect when strain, traffic, flow, quality, yield, efficiency, precision or pressure changes. Continuous anomaly detection for sensor telemetry and digital data streams.

- Dashboard: {{ frontend_base_url }}/

## At a glance

| Workspace | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| Sources | Connect MQTT sources and choose which readings to record |
| Monitoring | Design and run visual detection pipelines |
| Reporting | Inspect changes alongside their readings and detection evidence |
| Dojo | Replay recorded data, compare runs and refine a design |

The hero cycles through generated photographs of a steel bridge, a data-centre cable runway, an industrial pipe rack, a powered roller conveyor, a solar canopy, an industrial cooling bank, an overhead robotic gantry and a centre-pivot irrigation span. These illustrate connected infrastructure; they are not photographs of Aberration hardware or customer installations. The product screenshot shows the actual Reporting interface with illustrative cooling-circuit data, not customer telemetry or a performance benchmark. The example monitoring flow is a simplified explanation, not a screenshot.

## From raw signal. To informed decision.

Bring your readings into one workspace. Build monitors around your system, investigate the changes they flag, and test your next design on recorded data.

## See the change. Keep the context.

Inspect a detected change alongside the readings behind it. Follow the original signal and the detection evidence on a shared timeline, then zoom in on the period that matters.

## Build a monitor around your system.

A single reading or a set of related signals. A fixed reference or a baseline that adapts. Shape the detection to the way your system behaves.

- **Choose what to collect.** Connect MQTT sources, organise their readings, and decide what to record. Keep the history you need for monitoring and investigation.
- **Make the logic visible.** Prepare and combine signals, choose detection steps, and connect them in a visual pipeline. Save a design to use again.
- **Follow it as it runs.** See monitor status and review the evidence as new readings arrive. Move from a flagged change back to the underlying data.

An example monitoring flow: outlet pressure and flow rate → prepare and combine → learn and detect → collect the evidence. Designed visually, applied as the data arrives.

## Try it on the past. Before it watches the present.

Replay recorded readings or a CSV through a detection pipeline in the Dojo. See what it would have flagged, compare your options, and take a tested design into monitoring.

1. **Replay — give a design some history.** Choose recorded data or upload a dataset. Replay it in time order through the same detection logic used in live monitoring.
2. **Compare — find out what it catches.** Label known events and compare runs. Look at detected changes, detection delays, and false alarms together.
3. **Refine — take the better design forward.** Adjust the pipeline, run it again, and reuse the design in monitoring when you are ready.

Recorded data → test and compare → live monitoring. One workflow. Room to improve.
